## Why This Is Asked
Tests handling of drift in a multi-tenant streaming system with region isolation, ensuring containment without collateral impact. It also probes data lineage, governance, and rollout discipline.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with tenant scope, region, evidence, ownership, and lineage
- Region-tenant gated lifecycle to prevent cross-tenant bleed
- Minimal REST API for CAPA creation/update with linked logs/traces
- Tenant-scoped metrics: containment MTTR, drift score, recurrence rate, false positives
- RCA template and canary rollback plan for sessionization logic changes

## Code Example
```javascript
// Skeleton: CAPA data model (conceptual)
class CAPA {
  constructor(id, tenantId, region) {
    this.id = id;
    this.tenantId = tenantId;
    this.region = region;
    this.evidence = [];
    this.rootCause = null;
    this.actions = [];
    this.owner = null;
    this.state = 'DETECTED';
    this.timestamps = {};
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test isolation between tenants during a canary rollout?
- How would you handle cross-tenant drift if tenants share resources?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end CAPA design for a real multi-tenant data routing bug with data residency risk, ensuring traceability from detection to verification and rollback.

## Key Concepts
- CAPA data model with evidence, tenant IDs, and route metadata
- Region-aware lifecycle state machine with clear escalations
- Linked logs/traces in CAPA records for auditability
- Canary rollout and RCA templates with measurable metrics

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal REST API skeleton for CAPA creation
app.post('/capa', (req, res) => {
  const { evidence, tenantId, routeInfo } = req.body;
  // persist CAPA and link logs/traces
  res.status(201).send({ id: 'capa-123', status: 'open' });
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify false positives and tune thresholds across regions?
- What automated checks would prevent reintroduction of the misrouting in future deployments?
